  {
   "type": "faq",
   "h": "Questions validation and engineering teams ask",
   "items": [
    {
     "q": "Does a WFI loop have to be hot?",
     "a": "Not by rule. Annex 1 paragraph 6.10 gives constant circulation above 70 °C as an example of storage that minimizes microbial growth, not the only acceptable method. Ambient WFI loops with ozone or periodic heat sanitization are in use, but your contamination control strategy has to justify the choice and your monitoring data has to support it. Where a still delivers hot WFI, a hot loop is usually the simplest to defend; with membrane WFI, the energy case often favours ambient storage. We set out both for your demand."
    },
    {
     "q": "Which L/D do you design to, and how do you measure it?",
     "a": "L/D ≤ 2, the ASME BPE target: L from the inside wall of the main pipe to the valve seal point, D the inside diameter of the branch. It applies to instrument tees and sample valves as well as points of use. The older six-diameter rule comes from the FDA's 1993 inspection guide, which notes it was developed for hot 75–80 °C systems; we do not design to it. Every branch is listed with its L/D in the dead leg register."
    },
    {
     "q": "How do you keep flow turbulent when several points of use open at once?",
     "a": "We take the worst-case combination of outlets from your survey, not the average, and size the pipe and pump so the return stays turbulent with all of them open. A variable speed pump holds return flow or pressure as demand changes, and a low-return-flow alarm tells you when it cannot. At SAT we open the worst-case outlets together and record the return flow, which gives you the qualified flow rate Annex 1, 6.9 asks you to monitor."
    },
    {
     "q": "Ozone or periodic heat for an ambient PW loop?",
     "a": "Ozone keeps the tank under continuous control and sanitizes the loop without heat cycles or insulated piping, at the cost of ozone and UV monitoring, ozone-compatible seals and an extra test after each sanitization. Periodic heat needs no residual test but takes the loop out of use for each cycle and needs heating capacity. Your utilities, your tolerance for downtime and any existing equipment usually decide it; we show both against your schedule."
    },
    {
     "q": "How do you prove there is no ozone at the point of use?",
     "a": "An ozone sensor after the UV destruct unit is interlocked to stop supply if ozone breaks through, and the UV intensity is monitored. After each loop sanitization the water is tested for residual ozone before the loop returns to use, in line with Annex 1, 6.12, which asks for water to be tested after disinfection. The interlock is tested at FAT and OQ."
    },
    {
     "q": "How should points of use be sampled?",
     "a": "The way the water is used. The FDA inspection guide says that if a hose is usually attached, the sample should be taken at the end of the hose, and flush times should match production practice. Annex 1, 6.13 asks for a sampling programme that covers all outlets and points of use at a specified interval, with at least one representative sample every day of water used in manufacturing. We place sample valves for the return and the tank, and write point-of-use sampling into the PQ protocol."
    },
    {
     "q": "What about rouge?",
     "a": "Rouge, the iron oxide film that forms on stainless steel, is found mainly in hot WFI and pure steam systems and in systems with frequent heat cycles. On its own it does not usually move conductivity or TOC, but loose rouge that migrates is a particle risk, and your quality unit will expect it to be inspected and trended. We limit the causes at design (finish, passivation, chloride control during construction, pump selection), give you inspection points at the tank and pump, and plan derouging and repassivation as maintenance rather than an emergency."
    },
    {
     "q": "Can you add points of use to an existing loop?",
     "a": "Yes, after a hydraulic check of the whole loop at the new peak draw. Each new branch is cut in with a zero-static valve, orbitally welded, passivated and added to the isometrics and dead leg register, under your change control, with a requalification scope agreed before work starts. Shutdown length depends on the tie-ins: [typical shutdown for a point-of-use addition, days]. See upgrades and requalification."
    },
    {
     "q": "Can the loop connect to a generator from another supplier?",
     "a": "Yes. We define the interface in the functional specification: water quality and flow at the tank inlet, the signals exchanged, and how a generator divert or a low tank level is handled, so the loop and the generator are qualified as one system."
    },
    {
     "q": "How do you document slope, orbital welds and borescope inspection in the turnover package?",
     "a": "Slope is recorded line by line on the as-built isometric drawings, [slope verification method]. Every weld has a line in the weld log tied to the weld map and the material heat numbers, with [borescope inspection percentage] borescope inspection recorded. The Fabrication page shows each field and what it proves."
    },
    {
     "q": "Who services the loop after handover?",
     "a": "Our service team, from [service locations]. A service plan covers vent filter replacement and integrity tests, instrument calibration, pump seals, UV lamps and rouge inspection, with a stated on-site response for a severity 1 fault of [severity 1 on-site response time, hours]. Every visit ends with a signed report your quality unit can file."
    }
